WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.627
Here's one number. 0.625.

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:07.002
Ask JavaScript to round it to two
decimal places, and you get 0.63.

00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:10.585
Ask Python the same thing, the
everyday way, and you get 0.62.

00:00:10.625 --> 00:00:12.625
Same number. Same request. Two answers.

00:00:12.792 --> 00:00:14.335
I had just rewritten a finance detector

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:16.333
from one of those
languages into the other.

00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:18.375
So the question I couldn't
put down was this.

00:00:18.625 --> 00:00:21.625
If my version had quietly done that
somewhere, would I have noticed?

00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:25.717
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:28.010
This week I worked on a
contribution to Mycroft,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.467
which describes itself as "both a book and
a working agentic repository" for finance.

00:00:32.758 --> 00:00:34.633
Its recipes are reusable pipelines.

00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:36.635
I picked one, a contradiction detector,

00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:39.927
and rewrote its core logic from the
original workflow's JavaScript into

00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:42.217
Python, so the recipe
has scripts that run.

00:00:42.592 --> 00:00:44.217
This video is about one thing.

00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:47.593
How I checked that my version
still says the same thing.

00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:49.258
It came down to three matches.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:53.350
Here's what it does. It reads five
kinds of evidence about a company.

00:00:53.475 --> 00:00:56.102
The guidance it gives on earnings
calls, the risks it admits,

00:00:56.142 --> 00:00:59.808
the questions analysts pressed on, news
sentiment, and engineering activity.

00:01:00.142 --> 00:01:02.393
Six patterns look for places
where two of those disagree,

00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:05.475
and every disagreement becomes
a flag for a person to review.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.808
To test it, I invented sixteen companies.

00:01:07.892 --> 00:01:09.977
Every name ends in "Fixture Co."

00:01:10.017 --> 00:01:12.308
No real company is judged in this video.

00:01:12.983 --> 00:01:15.483
The first match is my own
reading against the original.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:18.567
Before writing a single line of
Python, I did something slower.

00:01:18.942 --> 00:01:21.527
I went through the original
JavaScript, pattern by pattern,

00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:24.942
and wrote down the flags it should raise
for each of those sixteen companies.

00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:27.067
Thirteen flags, written down first.

00:01:27.108 --> 00:01:30.150
Because if I write the expected
answers after my code exists,

00:01:30.192 --> 00:01:33.317
I'll just write down whatever
my code says, mistakes included.

00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:36.117
The second match is the real comparison.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.117
My parity check takes the original

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.200
JavaScript straight out
of the workflow file.

00:01:40.242 --> 00:01:42.993
It runs it right beside my Python,
on the same sixteen companies,

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:45.075
and compares the two, field by field.

00:01:45.117 --> 00:01:47.908
Every flag, every sentence
of evidence, word for word.

00:01:47.992 --> 00:01:50.993
Sixteen out of sixteen agreed. And
that felt good for about a minute.

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:52.492
Then it started to bother me.

00:01:52.575 --> 00:01:55.367
A match only tells you something
if a mismatch could have shown up.

00:01:55.742 --> 00:01:57.867
So where could these two actually differ?

00:01:58.625 --> 00:02:01.668
Some places, I knew about. JavaScript
and Python don't always agree.

00:02:01.708 --> 00:02:06.502
That rounding tie is one. Another:
JavaScript prints 1.0 as just "one,"

00:02:06.542 --> 00:02:08.627
while Python prints "1.0."

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:12.168
And a fallback written with two vertical
bars keeps an empty list in JavaScript,

00:02:12.208 --> 00:02:14.125
where Python's "or" skips past it.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:16.208
A flag's wording carries
numbers like these,

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:19.917
so I made my Python copy JavaScript's
behavior on each one, on purpose.

00:02:20.675 --> 00:02:22.550
Here's the part I want to be honest about.

00:02:22.592 --> 00:02:24.552
To see whether my comparison
would notice a difference,

00:02:24.592 --> 00:02:26.633
I made small changes to my own copy.

00:02:26.842 --> 00:02:30.718
I moved one threshold from 0.6 to 0.61.

00:02:30.758 --> 00:02:32.552
I swapped in Python's own rounding.

00:02:32.592 --> 00:02:34.550
And the comparison said
everything still agreed.

00:02:34.592 --> 00:02:38.008
It was doing its job. My test
companies just never stood on the line.

00:02:38.092 --> 00:02:43.092
Not one of them sat at exactly 0.6, and
no news average landed exactly on a tie.

00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:46.600
So I added two companies
built to stand exactly there.

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:51.393
Romeo Fixture Co gives guidance
at a confidence of exactly 0.6.

00:02:51.433 --> 00:02:55.685
Quebec Fixture Co has a news
average of exactly 0.625.

00:02:55.725 --> 00:02:59.227
Run the same two changes again, and each
one fails on the company placed for it.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:01.975
The threshold, on Romeo.
The rounding, on Quebec.

00:03:02.017 --> 00:03:04.725
The fix wasn't new code.
It was better test data.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.525
The third match was closer to
home, because both sides were mine.

00:03:08.942 --> 00:03:12.360
At the last review, I put the human
report side by side with its machine log,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.275
for the run that's designed
to stop early on bad data.

00:03:15.358 --> 00:03:17.483
The report said thirteen
rows were rejected.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:21.608
The log said zero. Same run, two different
stories, and I wrote both of them.

00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:24.650
The log was only counting a later
step, one that never got to run.

00:03:24.858 --> 00:03:27.775
Mycroft's own rules say
no artifact silently wins.

00:03:28.192 --> 00:03:30.233
So now one list feeds both files,

00:03:30.317 --> 00:03:35.150
anything that wasn't checked says "not
checked," and a new test compares the two.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.492
Everything you've seen
ran on invented data.

00:03:37.658 --> 00:03:40.992
No database, no news feed, and
no AI model was ever called.

00:03:41.283 --> 00:03:44.993
Live data, the model's answers, what
happens when a real service times out:

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:48.993
none of that is tested. So at the approval
step, I declined live mode, on the record,

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:51.408
and wrote down what would
need to be true first.

00:03:51.533 --> 00:03:53.950
That call belongs to the
people with live access.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:58.208
And because this is a shared
repository, I held myself to one rule.

00:03:58.375 --> 00:04:01.875
Add, never remove. Every change
is a new file for this one recipe,

00:04:01.958 --> 00:04:04.208
or an update to this recipe's own page.

00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:07.333
Plus a few lines added to
the end of two shared files.

00:04:07.375 --> 00:04:09.292
Nothing deleted. Nothing renamed.

00:04:10.133 --> 00:04:14.052
So if you've ever rewritten something you
depend on, in a new language, a new tool,

00:04:14.092 --> 00:04:17.258
or just a cleaner version, here's
the question I'd leave you with.

00:04:17.342 --> 00:04:19.758
Where would your two versions
most likely disagree?

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:23.883
Find that one spot, and make sure a
single test is standing exactly on it.

00:04:24.558 --> 00:04:26.308
Same Logic, Two Languages.

00:04:26.558 --> 00:04:28.977
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for Humanitarians AI.

00:04:29.017 --> 00:04:30.267
Signing off.
